AWS-007 — Workflow Orchestration

Document ID: AWS-007
Title: Workflow Orchestration
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Orchestration Model (CWOM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow Orchestration Model establishes the principles, architecture, coordination mechanisms, synchronization semantics, and governance required to coordinate multiple Workflow Definitions and Workflow Instances into coherent engineering execution systems while preserving determinism, traceability, and engineering intent.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow orchestration architecture
Workflow coordination
Dependencies
Synchronization
Event-driven orchestration
Distributed execution
Compensation workflows
Orchestration governance

This specification applies to every orchestrated workflow system.

3. Canonical Definition

Workflow Orchestration is the governed coordination of multiple Workflow Instances and supporting execution resources to achieve a higher-level engineering objective while preserving workflow autonomy, governance, and execution integrity.

Orchestration coordinates workflows.

It SHALL NOT redefine individual workflow semantics.

4. Objectives

The Workflow Orchestration Model SHALL:

Coordinate multiple workflows.
Preserve workflow independence.
Enable distributed execution.
Support deterministic coordination.
Enable recovery.
Preserve traceability.
Support federation.
Enable scalable execution.
5. Orchestration Principles

Every workflow orchestration SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit Coordination

Workflow coordination SHALL be explicitly defined.

Implicit orchestration SHALL NOT exist.

Workflow Autonomy

Each Workflow Instance SHALL preserve its own execution semantics.

Deterministic Coordination

Equivalent orchestrations SHALL produce equivalent coordination behavior.

Traceability

Every orchestration decision SHALL remain permanently traceable.

Governed Coordination

Applicable Canonical Policy Objects SHALL govern orchestration.

Recoverability

Orchestration SHALL support coordinated recovery.

